Relevant columns and their possible usage  

Match  
• league_id: to group results by league  
• season: to confirm that “all seasons” are covered (no filter needed)  
• home_team_api_id / away_team_api_id: to identify the two teams that played a match  
• home_team_goal / away_team_goal: compare these two integers to decide which team (if any) won the match  

Team  
• team_api_id: primary‑key that matches the *_team_api_id columns in Match  
• team_long_name / team_short_name: readable team names that will be shown in the final answer  

League  
• id: joins to Match.league_id  
• name: readable league name to be returned in the final answer  

Logical conditions that will be useful  

• Home win  →  home_team_goal > away_team_goal  
• Away win  →  away_team_goal > home_team_goal  
• Draw      →  home_team_goal = away_team_goal (zero wins added for either side)  

-- — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — — —  
Below are 10 exploratory‑to‑final SQL queries (all limited to 20 rows except the final answer) that progressively build toward the requested result.  
Use them one‑by‑one to understand the data; each carries its own short description.

```sql
-- 1) Look at the first 20 rows of Match to see the goal columns
SELECT id, league_id, season, home_team_api_id, away_team_api_id,
       home_team_goal, away_team_goal
FROM   Match
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 2) List leagues that appear in Match together with their names
SELECT DISTINCT m.league_id, l.name AS league_name
FROM   Match AS m
JOIN   League AS l ON l.id = m.league_id
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 3) Show 20 matches that ended in a home win
SELECT id, home_team_api_id, away_team_api_id, home_team_goal, away_team_goal
FROM   Match
WHERE  home_team_goal > away_team_goal
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 4) Show 20 matches that ended in an away win
SELECT id, home_team_api_id, away_team_api_id, home_team_goal, away_team_goal
FROM   Match
WHERE  away_team_goal > home_team_goal
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 5) Calculate one row per match indicating the winning team_api_id
SELECT id AS match_id,
       CASE
         WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN home_team_api_id
         WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN away_team_api_id
       END AS winner_team_api_id
FROM   Match
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 6) Count total wins for every team in the entire data set
SELECT winner_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
       COUNT(*) AS wins
FROM (
      SELECT CASE
               WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN home_team_api_id
               WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN away_team_api_id
             END AS winner_team_api_id
      FROM   Match
)
WHERE  winner_team_api_id IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY winner_team_api_id
ORDER  BY wins ASC
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 7) Count wins per team *per league*
SELECT m.league_id,
       t.team_api_id,
       COUNT(*) AS wins
FROM   Match AS m
JOIN   (
        -- derive winner for each match
        SELECT id,
               CASE
                 WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN home_team_api_id
                 WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN away_team_api_id
               END AS winner_team_api_id
        FROM   Match
       ) AS w ON w.id = m.id
JOIN   Team AS t ON t.team_api_id = w.winner_team_api_id
WHERE  w.winner_team_api_id IS NOT NULL
GROUP  BY m.league_id, t.team_api_id
ORDER  BY m.league_id, wins ASC
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 8) Add the teams that have *zero* wins in each league
--    (left join all teams that ever played in the league)
WITH teams_in_league AS (
     SELECT league_id, home_team_api_id AS team_api_id FROM Match
     UNION
     SELECT league_id, away_team_api_id FROM Match
),
wins AS (
     SELECT m.league_id,
            CASE
              WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN home_team_api_id
              WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN away_team_api_id
            END AS winner_team_api_id
     FROM   Match AS m
),
wins_per_team AS (
     SELECT league_id,
            winner_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
            COUNT(*) AS wins
     FROM   wins
     WHERE  winner_team_api_id IS NOT NULL
     GROUP  BY league_id, winner_team_api_id
)
SELECT til.league_id,
       til.team_api_id,
       COALESCE(wpt.wins,0) AS wins
FROM   teams_in_league AS til
LEFT   JOIN wins_per_team AS wpt
       ON  wpt.league_id = til.league_id
       AND wpt.team_api_id = til.team_api_id
ORDER  BY til.league_id, wins
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 9) For every league, find the *minimum* number of wins
WITH teams_in_league AS (
     SELECT league_id, home_team_api_id AS team_api_id FROM Match
     UNION
     SELECT league_id, away_team_api_id FROM Match
),
wins AS (
     SELECT m.league_id,
            CASE
              WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN home_team_api_id
              WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN away_team_api_id
            END AS winner_team_api_id
     FROM   Match AS m
),
wins_per_team AS (
     SELECT league_id,
            winner_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
            COUNT(*) AS wins
     FROM   wins
     WHERE  winner_team_api_id IS NOT NULL
     GROUP  BY league_id, winner_team_api_id
),
all_wins AS (
     SELECT til.league_id,
            til.team_api_id,
            COALESCE(wpt.wins,0) AS wins
     FROM   teams_in_league AS til
     LEFT   JOIN wins_per_team AS wpt
            ON  wpt.league_id = til.league_id
            AND wpt.team_api_id = til.team_api_id
)
SELECT league_id,
       MIN(wins) AS fewest_wins
FROM   all_wins
GROUP  BY league_id
LIMIT  20;
```

```sql
-- 10) FINAL ANSWER:
--     For each league, return *one* team that owns the fewest total wins
--     (ties broken arbitrarily by the smallest team_api_id).
WITH teams_in_league AS (
     SELECT league_id, home_team_api_id AS team_api_id FROM Match
     UNION
     SELECT league_id, away_team_api_id FROM Match
),
wins AS (
     SELECT m.league_id,
            CASE
              WHEN home_team_goal > away_team_goal THEN home_team_api_id
              WHEN away_team_goal > home_team_goal THEN away_team_api_id
            END AS winner_team_api_id
     FROM   Match AS m
),
wins_per_team AS (
     SELECT league_id,
            winner_team_api_id AS team_api_id,
            COUNT(*) AS wins
     FROM   wins
     WHERE  winner_team_api_id IS NOT NULL
     GROUP  BY league_id, winner_team_api_id
),
all_wins AS (
     SELECT til.league_id,
            til.team_api_id,
            COALESCE(wpt.wins,0) AS wins
     FROM   teams_in_league AS til
     LEFT   JOIN wins_per_team AS wpt
            ON  wpt.league_id = til.league_id
            AND wpt.team_api_id = til.team_api_id
),
fewest AS (
     SELECT league_id,
            MIN(wins) AS fewest_wins
     FROM   all_wins
     GROUP  BY league_id
)
SELECT le.name  AS league_name,
       te.team_long_name AS team_name,
       aw.wins          AS total_wins
FROM   fewest AS f
JOIN   all_wins AS aw
       ON aw.league_id = f.league_id
      AND aw.wins      = f.fewest_wins
JOIN   League  AS le ON le.id  = aw.league_id
JOIN   Team    AS te ON te.team_api_id = aw.team_api_id
-- break any remaining ties deterministically
GROUP  BY le.id
HAVING te.team_api_id = MIN(te.team_api_id);
```

The 10th query yields exactly one row per league: the league name, the team with the fewest total wins (including zero‑win teams), and the number of wins, satisfying the stated requirements.
